1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,319
Lesson 4.5: GBIs and Problem Note.

2
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:14,160
For a scratch patient, the last piece of work in ThoroughCare is the care plan,

3
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:18,288
and the problem note we formulated in PointClickCare.

4
00:00:18,338 --> 00:00:20,200
The care plan rests on three parts,

5
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:25,212
often called the GBIs: goals, barriers and interventions.

6
00:00:25,262 --> 00:00:27,294
Goals describe what we want to achieve,

7
00:00:27,334 --> 00:00:33,483
as SMART goals that fit the condition and the patient. Every patient gets at least two.

8
00:00:33,533 --> 00:00:35,521
Barriers explain what could get in the way:

9
00:00:35,561 --> 00:00:39,583
at least two linked to the condition, and two general ones.

10
00:00:39,633 --> 00:00:45,495
And interventions are the care manager actions that move the goals forward and tackle the barriers.

11
00:00:45,535 --> 00:00:47,670
Again, at least two.

12
00:00:49,833 --> 00:00:52,830
All of this work happens under the Care Management tab,

13
00:00:52,870 --> 00:00:55,652
where the care plan is split into subtabs.

14
00:00:55,702 --> 00:00:59,901
The clinical review is already done, so our next stop is Problems.

15
00:01:02,633 --> 00:01:06,660
ThoroughCare has already created two problems from the data we entered:

16
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:09,700
hyperlipidemia, and gaps in care.

17
00:01:10,633 --> 00:01:12,466
We don't use these condition templates,

18
00:01:12,506 --> 00:01:15,744
so we cancel both and keep the care plan clean.

19
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:21,863
Opening hyperlipidemia

20
00:01:21,903 --> 00:01:26,489
and scrolling to the bottom of the page brings us to the Cancel button.

21
00:01:26,539 --> 00:01:30,930
Cancelling removes the problem, and gaps in care goes the same way.

22
00:01:35,233 --> 00:01:40,193
Now the Problems section is empty, ready for the care plan we actually use.

23
00:01:42,520 --> 00:01:46,043
Clicking the plus button opens the Add Problem window.

24
00:01:51,967 --> 00:01:56,225
We use one fixed template for every patient: SNF Care Plan,

25
00:01:56,265 --> 00:02:00,581
because all our patients live in skilled nursing facilities.

26
00:02:01,533 --> 00:02:05,252
The first two sections, desired outcomes and long-term goals,

27
00:02:05,292 --> 00:02:07,593
stay empty. We skip them.

28
00:02:14,167 --> 00:02:17,465
The three sections that follow are the heart of the care plan:

29
00:02:17,505 --> 00:02:20,853
SMART goals, interventions, and barriers.

30
00:02:23,233 --> 00:02:26,092
SMART goals have to fit two things: the condition,

31
00:02:26,132 --> 00:02:30,720
here hyperlipidemia, and the patient's actual situation.

32
00:02:31,867 --> 00:02:34,774
To see which goals fit, we turn to the Care Policy.

33
00:02:34,814 --> 00:02:40,758
Its Conditions Guide lists goals, barriers and interventions for each condition.

34
00:02:43,467 --> 00:02:45,435
Each goal comes with a reason to choose it.

35
00:02:45,475 --> 00:02:53,232
Exercise goals suit a patient who is physically inactive, with no physical or occupational therapy.

36
00:02:53,282 --> 00:02:56,629
A blood pressure goal fits when blood pressure is uncontrolled:

37
00:02:56,669 --> 00:03:04,438
systolic above 140 or below 90, or diastolic above 90 or below 60.

38
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:06,694
Hemoglobin A1C works the same way.

39
00:03:06,734 --> 00:03:12,114
Say the latest A1C was 10.5, drawn on October 1.

40
00:03:12,167 --> 00:03:16,885
We check A1C every three months, so the next recheck falls on January 1,

41
00:03:16,925 --> 00:03:21,432
and the goal is a better value by then, maybe 7 or 8.

42
00:03:21,482 --> 00:03:27,999
Cholesterol, LDL, blood sugar, triglycerides and HDL goals follow the same logic,

43
00:03:28,039 --> 00:03:31,386
whenever the last reading was out of range.

44
00:03:31,436 --> 00:03:35,933
After comparing the list with the patient's data, we pick at least two goals.

45
00:03:44,167 --> 00:03:45,311
Back in ThoroughCare,

46
00:03:45,351 --> 00:03:50,552
typing hemoglobin into the New SMART Goal field brings up the matching goals.

47
00:03:50,602 --> 00:03:52,956
Here is the one we found in the Care Policy:

48
00:03:52,996 --> 00:03:58,765
lower my hemoglobin A1C to less than a target percent, by next recheck.

49
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:06,317
Filling in the blanks turns it into this patient's goal:

50
00:04:06,356 --> 00:04:11,127
below 9 percent, by January 1, 2027.

51
00:04:11,177 --> 00:04:15,741
Every patient needs at least two goals, so a second one follows the same method,

52
00:04:15,781 --> 00:04:19,134
matched to what the patient actually needs.

53
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,013
We'll come back to interventions. Barriers come first,

54
00:04:23,053 --> 00:04:28,166
because they shape what we do: what could prevent us from resolving this problem?

55
00:04:35,667 --> 00:04:38,886
The Conditions Guide lists the barriers for each condition,

56
00:04:38,926 --> 00:04:41,203
right beside the SMART goals.

57
00:04:41,267 --> 00:04:45,393
Each one is checked against the patient's data. Diet adherence issues,

58
00:04:45,433 --> 00:04:49,499
for example, came up while we formulated the problem note.

59
00:04:49,549 --> 00:04:52,363
Current tobacco use shows up in the progress notes,

60
00:04:52,403 --> 00:04:56,832
with searches like tobacco, cigarettes, smoking or smoker.

61
00:04:56,882 --> 00:05:00,996
Physical inactivity applies when the patient isn't in any therapy,

62
00:05:01,036 --> 00:05:03,440
physical or occupational.

63
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:08,676
A knowledge deficit about self-monitoring fits a patient who refuses monitoring,

64
00:05:08,716 --> 00:05:12,594
so vitals like blood pressure aren't checked regularly.

65
00:05:12,644 --> 00:05:17,725
Medication adherence issues come straight from the adherence check we already did.

66
00:05:17,775 --> 00:05:22,496
And an unmanaged comorbid condition means another condition isn't controlled,

67
00:05:22,536 --> 00:05:25,691
like diabetes with a high A1C.

68
00:05:25,741 --> 00:05:28,986
We pick at least two of these condition-specific barriers.

69
00:05:35,933 --> 00:05:36,830
In ThoroughCare,

70
00:05:36,870 --> 00:05:42,127
checking Diet adherence issues and Physical inactivity adds them as barriers.

71
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:46,850
Then come at least two general barriers from this list,

72
00:05:46,890 --> 00:05:49,415
whichever apply to the patient.

73
00:05:51,067 --> 00:05:55,445
Impaired mobility, for instance, rests on the walking items we reviewed,

74
00:05:55,485 --> 00:05:59,955
10, 50 and 150 feet, and on the MDS.

75
00:06:04,767 --> 00:06:08,934
Altered cognitive function is verified in the MDS as well,

76
00:06:08,974 --> 00:06:15,431
so we open the MDS tab in PointClickCare and its latest completed assessment.

77
00:06:21,333 --> 00:06:24,488
The MDS covers the patient's basic functions:

78
00:06:24,528 --> 00:06:28,500
mobility, activities of daily living, and cognition.

79
00:06:30,167 --> 00:06:32,760
For cognition, every patient takes the BIMS,

80
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:35,511
the Brief Interview for Mental Status.

81
00:06:35,567 --> 00:06:38,306
It asks about time and place, short-term memory,

82
00:06:38,346 --> 00:06:42,919
and recalling new information, and gives a score out of 15.

83
00:06:42,969 --> 00:06:45,810
13 to 15 means cognition is intact.

84
00:06:45,850 --> 00:06:51,806
8 to 12 points to a moderate decline, and 7 or below to a severe one.

85
00:06:51,867 --> 00:06:54,567
This patient scored 9, a moderate decline,

86
00:06:54,607 --> 00:06:58,505
so altered cognitive function is a fitting barrier.

87
00:07:02,233 --> 00:07:07,945
Behavioral health issues apply when the patient has an active psychiatric diagnosis,

88
00:07:07,985 --> 00:07:13,100
such as depression, anxiety, schizophrenia or bipolar disorder.

89
00:07:17,433 --> 00:07:20,218
The MED DIAG tab in PointClickCare answers that.

90
00:07:20,258 --> 00:07:24,458
This patient has depression, so it joins the barriers.

91
00:07:29,300 --> 00:07:31,448
By now, the plan has at least two goals,

92
00:07:31,488 --> 00:07:35,145
two barriers for the condition, and two general ones.

93
00:07:41,233 --> 00:07:43,017
Interventions answer the question:

94
00:07:43,057 --> 00:07:48,125
how do we achieve those goals? They build on the goals and barriers we just set.

95
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:56,763
The section has three tabs and opens on Patient Actions,

96
00:07:56,803 --> 00:08:01,352
which we don't use. Care Manager Actions is the one we need.

97
00:08:01,402 --> 00:08:05,767
It offers 11 ready-made care manager actions to choose from.

98
00:08:09,033 --> 00:08:13,173
The Care Policy also lists care manager actions for each condition,

99
00:08:13,213 --> 00:08:15,559
next to its goals and barriers.

100
00:08:19,333 --> 00:08:22,263
They're picked to match the barriers. An active smoker,

101
00:08:22,303 --> 00:08:26,497
for example, calls for smoking cessation education.

102
00:08:26,547 --> 00:08:31,534
An uncontrolled lipid profile calls for education on the types of cholesterol

103
00:08:31,574 --> 00:08:33,416
and their goal levels.

104
00:08:33,467 --> 00:08:38,039
And diet adherence issues call for reviewing foods high in cholesterol to avoid

105
00:08:38,079 --> 00:08:39,665
or limit.

106
00:08:43,433 --> 00:08:44,578
Back in ThoroughCare,

107
00:08:44,618 --> 00:08:48,738
searching for smoking cessation adds that action to the plan.

108
00:08:52,167 --> 00:08:55,999
Reviewing foods high in cholesterol goes in the same way.

109
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:05,240
Actions from the predefined list can join too, whenever they fit the patient.

110
00:09:06,567 --> 00:09:09,177
Before saving, the second SMART goal goes in:

111
00:09:09,217 --> 00:09:12,673
exercise, 60 minutes, 3 days a week.

112
00:09:14,867 --> 00:09:17,959
The rule for every patient: at least two SMART goals,

113
00:09:17,999 --> 00:09:21,574
two care manager actions, and two barriers.

114
00:09:24,272 --> 00:09:28,535
With all three in place, saving creates the SNF care plan.

115
00:09:31,567 --> 00:09:34,637
The SNF Care Plan now sits under Problems.

116
00:09:34,677 --> 00:09:39,529
Opening it shows the goals, interventions and barriers we chose.

117
00:09:44,133 --> 00:09:46,434
At the bottom is the Problem Notes box:

118
00:09:46,474 --> 00:09:51,244
this is where the problem note we formulated from PointClickCare goes.

119
00:09:56,933 --> 00:09:59,271
Before saving, it's worth a careful read:

120
00:09:59,311 --> 00:10:02,911
no inaccuracies, and no text that doesn't belong.

121
00:10:05,633 --> 00:10:07,384
Gaps in care should read clearly,

122
00:10:07,424 --> 00:10:10,684
so anyone can understand exactly what happened.

123
00:10:10,734 --> 00:10:14,781
A gap about spirometry testing for COPD, for example,

124
00:10:14,821 --> 00:10:18,920
tells the reader the patient hasn't had a spirometry test.

125
00:10:22,167 --> 00:10:26,477
Every relevant section should be there, including patient education.

126
00:10:28,133 --> 00:10:35,555
And the note always ends with our fixed sentence: this care plan has been reviewed and shared with the facility's medical team,

127
00:10:35,595 --> 00:10:40,591
nursing staff, and any direct caregiver involved in the patient's care.

128
00:10:47,033 --> 00:10:50,028
Saving posts the note under Previous Updates.

129
00:10:51,933 --> 00:10:55,610
If something needs fixing later, the edit button reopens the note,

130
00:10:55,650 --> 00:10:58,414
so we can correct it and save again.

131
00:11:03,233 --> 00:11:08,748
To wrap up: we cancelled the suggested problems and added the SNF Care Plan.

132
00:11:08,798 --> 00:11:13,879
We chose at least two SMART goals that fit both the condition and the patient.

133
00:11:13,929 --> 00:11:17,584
We added two barriers linked to the condition and two general ones,

134
00:11:17,624 --> 00:11:20,205
each backed by the patient's data.

135
00:11:20,255 --> 00:11:23,969
We picked at least two care manager actions that answer those goals

136
00:11:24,009 --> 00:11:25,306
and barriers.

137
00:11:25,356 --> 00:11:29,124
And we added the problem note, read it carefully, and saved it.

138
00:11:31,267 --> 00:11:35,728
Next, we log the encounter time and export the encounter file.
